        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "DSL Query Generation"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1765217226649"
          },
          "code": "DSL_QUERY_GENERATION",
          "id": "1765217181824",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1765217181825_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "pProductKeyword: {{$context.$nodes.EXTRACTORDERINFO.$output.searchKeyWord}} \n DSL Query Generation\n**Purpose:** Using the extracted `pProductKeyword`, generate Elasticsearch clauses for semantic search using the **full logic** below. Strictly follow each rule - do not skip or alter any instruction.\n\n**Elastic Search DSL Generation Rules** \nThe input `pProductKeyword` should be used as the search text.\n**STRICTLY DO NOT USE**  any other product related inputs like `pProductCriteria`\n\nFields:\nItem.Description\nItem.LongDescription\n\nExact Match Field:\nItem.ItemNumber.raw\n\nRules:\n\n- Tokenize the search text into words using standard English language rules (i.e., split based on spaces and punctuation). Do not use subword or model-internal tokenization—a single English word must be treated as a single token.\n- Estimate token importance using your language knowledge (e.g. rare/descriptive = higher boost). In addition, assign a significantly higher boost to tokens that are:\n       a. Concrete, object-based nouns (e.g. 'desktop', 'treadmill', 'bicycle'), and\n       b. Tokens that are likely misspellings of such nouns (e.g. 'machne' → 'machine'), using approximate string matching or phonetic similarity to infer the intended word. Prioritize these even if they are not in the vocabulary or appear invalid.\n- Add a `term` clause on Item.ItemNumber.raw using the full string, case-insensitive, boost 10.\n- Before adding any match_phrase clause, first check if the tokenized search text has more than one token.\n - Only if it does, add match_phrase clauses for the full text to all fields with:\n    - slop 0 -> boost 2\n    - slop 1 -> boost 15\n    - slop 2 -> boost 10\n    - slop 3 -> boost 5\n- Do not add any match_phrase clause at all if the tokenized search text has only one token.\n- Only if the tokenized search text has more than one token, add match_phrase (slop 1, boost 10) for adjacent token pairs.\n- Do not add this if the search text has only one word.\n- Add individual match clauses per token across all fields with inferred boost and fuzziness: 1, ensuring each boost is always lower than any match_phrase or term clause boost (i.e., less than 5).\n- Return all clauses as a JSON array only. Do not wrap in other DSL, do not include comments or additional explanations.\n- The clauses should be stored in `pShouldArray` as a JSON array:\n    pShouldArray: {value}\n- Only proceed once a valid `pShouldArray` is available."
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"properties\": {\n        \"pShouldArray\": {\n            \"type\": \"array\",\n            \"items\": {\n                \"type\": \"object\",\n                \"properties\": {}\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
